From: Yocto build fix
Subject: [PATCH] AndroidAutoEntity: set timestamp on outgoing PingRequest

AndroidAutoEntity::sendPing() sends a completely default-constructed
PingRequest, leaving its `timestamp` field (aasdk_proto's
PingRequestMessage.proto: `int64 timestamp = 1;`) at proto3's implicit
zero default. Modern Android Auto silently drops a ping with no valid
timestamp instead of answering it -- everything else in the session
(handshake, auth, service discovery, audio/video channel setup) works
fine, but pongsCount_ never advances, so after two 5s ping cycles
(~10s) Pinger::onTimerExceeded() rejects the promise, "ping timer
exceeded" fires, and AndroidAutoEntity tears the whole session down.

Set the timestamp to the current epoch time in milliseconds, same
convention used elsewhere in this codebase (AudioInputService.cpp,
InputService.cpp) for their AV timestamps.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [oe-specific: this device's RTC has no
battery backup so its wall clock is unreliable, and a fresh boot's
clock is arbitrary until/unless NTP has run; regardless, upstream
sends this field unset in a way real Android Auto clients don't
tolerate]

--- a/src/autoapp/Service/AndroidAutoEntity.cpp
+++ b/src/autoapp/Service/AndroidAutoEntity.cpp
@@ -19,6 +19,7 @@
 #include <f1x/aasdk/Channel/Control/ControlServiceChannel.hpp>
 #include <f1x/openauto/autoapp/Service/AndroidAutoEntity.hpp>
 #include <f1x/openauto/Common/Log.hpp>
+#include <chrono>

 namespace f1x
 {
@@ -272,6 +273,8 @@
     promise->then([]() {}, std::bind(&AndroidAutoEntity::onChannelError, this->shared_from_this(), std::placeholders::_1));

     aasdk::proto::messages::PingRequest request;
+    request.set_timestamp(std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::milliseconds>(
+        std::chrono::system_clock::now().time_since_epoch()).count());
     controlServiceChannel_->sendPingRequest(request, std::move(promise));
 }
